# Lunicle — local OAuth credentials.
#
# Copy to `.env` (which is gitignored) and fill in. The dev scripts source it;
# nothing else reads it. Production doesn't use this file at all — Railway holds
# the same two names as service Variables.
#
#   cp .env.example .env
#
# Where these come from: docs/oauth-instructions.html. Short version —
#
#   Google   one client covers both production and localhost, because Google
#            allows several JavaScript origins per client. So these two values
#            are the same ones Railway has.
#
# Leave these blank to disable sign-in locally; the server logs whether it is
# live at startup. Setting only one of the pair is a misconfiguration and the
# server will say so and disable sign-in.

LUNICLE_GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID=
LUNICLE_GOOGLE_CLIENT_SECRET=

# ── Transactional email (optional) ──────────────────────────────────────────
#
# The server sends notifications, sign-in codes and address-change codes through
# one of two transports. Configuring neither is a valid state: email is simply
# off and the server boots fine (sign-in by code is then unavailable). The
# server logs which transport is live at startup.
#
# LUNICLE_EMAIL_FROM is the From header for whichever transport is active — one
# shared address, e.g. `Lunicle <noreply@example.com>`. No per-transport from.
#
# Transport A — Resend (HTTPS API). Active when LUNICLE_RESEND_API_KEY is set;
# needs LUNICLE_EMAIL_FROM too (set both or neither).
#
#   LUNICLE_RESEND_API_KEY=
#   LUNICLE_EMAIL_FROM=
#
# Transport B — SMTP (a relay, a self-hosted MTA, or any SMTP provider). Active
# when LUNICLE_SMTP_HOST is set; needs a username, a password and
# LUNICLE_EMAIL_FROM as well (set the whole block or none of it). LUNICLE_SMTP_PORT
# defaults to 587 and LUNICLE_SMTP_TLS to `starttls`; use `tls` for implicit TLS on 465.
#
#   LUNICLE_SMTP_HOST=
#   LUNICLE_SMTP_PORT=587
#   LUNICLE_SMTP_USERNAME=
#   LUNICLE_SMTP_PASSWORD=
#   LUNICLE_SMTP_TLS=starttls
#
# If BOTH transports are fully configured, LUNICLE_EMAIL_TRANSPORT breaks the
# tie (`resend` or `smtp`). Omit it and the server warns and defaults to Resend.
#
#   LUNICLE_EMAIL_TRANSPORT=
#
# ── E-mail sign-in on/off (optional) ────────────────────────────────────────
#
# Whenever a transport above is configured, the server also offers signing in
# with a mailed one-time code. To keep e-mail for notifications and
# address-change codes but drop it as a SIGN-IN method — leaving Google OAuth as
# the only way in, e.g. a Workspace deployment — set this to `off`. Default is
# on, so leaving it unset keeps today's behaviour. It changes only the sign-in
# surfaces and the sign-in endpoints; notifications are unaffected.
#
#   LUNICLE_EMAIL_SIGN_IN=on
#
# Production sets these as Railway service Variables (the LUNICLE_ names above).
# To exercise email against a LOCAL mail server, pass the matching `-P`
# properties to the run task instead — they map to the same `-Dlunicle.*` the
# server reads before the environment (see server/build.gradle.kts):
#
#   ./gradlew :server:run \
#     -PsmtpHost=localhost -PsmtpPort=1025 \
#     -PsmtpUsername=dev -PsmtpPassword=dev -PsmtpTls=starttls \
#     -PemailFrom='Lunicle <noreply@localhost>'
